WEBVTT

00:21.849 --> 00:25.285
Okay. Hello, everybody. I'd like

00:25.301 --> 00:28.901
to welcome you to our November 10th Planning

00:28.933 --> 00:31.756
Commission meeting. This is, uh, we'll start with our workshop.

00:32.846 --> 00:36.583
Uh, just, uh, FYI, Commissioner Hess is

00:36.711 --> 00:39.357
excused tonight, so I guess I— You got me.

00:40.303 --> 00:43.142
You got me. So we'll go from there.

00:43.671 --> 00:48.049
Uh, on the discussion item, we have a concept

00:48.081 --> 00:51.994
plan for the bench, and Steve, I guess

00:52.107 --> 00:56.020
that's you, so let's go ahead and get started. Yeah, I guess

00:56.070 --> 00:56.404
so.

01:10.218 --> 01:11.743
So I'm not going to try to take too much time. I think I want

01:12.243 --> 01:14.192
to leave a lot of the time for the applicant to go over what they

01:14.692 --> 01:18.737
want to present, and if you'd ask them questions. Um, so just

01:19.237 --> 01:22.603
a brief review. This is one that first came to us as a master development

01:23.103 --> 01:26.553
plan proposal. However, the first step in that is they've got to submit a concept

01:27.053 --> 01:29.860
plan. So this was, um, resubmitted to us,

01:30.792 --> 01:34.099
uh, after some comments were made. So you can kind of— we'll go

01:34.599 --> 01:38.274
through them really quick. I don't want to— like I said,

01:38.290 --> 01:41.710
I don't really want to steal the applicant's thunder and make them— And

01:41.726 --> 01:45.002
I think you— I think I sent both a clean copy and one with staff

01:45.502 --> 01:48.092
comments on it. It was easier to do that than try to outline them all

01:48.592 --> 01:50.595
in the report. Sometimes it's easier just to give them context.

01:56.590 --> 02:00.610
So one of our biggest concerns here with this concept

02:01.110 --> 02:04.450
plan, probably the first one

02:04.547 --> 02:07.773
that I would bring up, is our code does require that any master development plan

02:08.273 --> 02:11.869
has to be 200 acres or less. The one they're proposing is over 1,800 acres.

02:13.225 --> 02:16.994
So what they could do as an option is present the concept plan, and then

02:17.494 --> 02:20.928
it would be split up into at least 9 different master development plans.

02:20.992 --> 02:24.589
So there'd be some cohesion still. However, there would still have

02:25.089 --> 02:27.094
to be separate ones that each have their own timelines, that each have their own

02:27.594 --> 02:31.076
benchmarks, and then work off of that. So that's one

02:31.172 --> 02:33.709
thing that'd have to be considered here.

02:35.187 --> 02:40.808
Overall, I do think we feel like it's a lot more units then

02:40.824 --> 02:44.617
really we're comfortable with staff. I don't know as far as the density and the

02:45.117 --> 02:48.196
placement of them. I just don't think it's a lot for one

02:48.659 --> 02:52.626
development. So just

02:52.772 --> 02:53.800
2 of those concerns there.

02:59.114 --> 03:02.319
Another one that we don't really want to see, as we've dealt with

03:02.819 --> 03:06.050
past master development plans, is we don't really want to see huge change

03:06.550 --> 03:08.572
in our standards. I do appreciate that they've tried to look at our,

03:10.198 --> 03:13.028
what our current zones are. Some of them match pretty closely.

03:14.400 --> 03:17.594
We kind of want to get away from doing huge master development plans where it's,

03:18.243 --> 03:20.724
they're their own thing, right? So we don't want the bench to have their own

03:20.821 --> 03:24.695
zone. We don't really want to see very unique standards. Like, we understand

03:25.195 --> 03:27.996
there may be some variation in some of our standards, but to see it to

03:28.496 --> 03:30.895
the scale of this, we're not very supportive of that.

03:33.306 --> 03:36.288
So, like I mentioned here, we'd like to just see our table put in,

03:36.514 --> 03:41.575
if that's what the case is, with very minor tweaks instead of pretty

03:48.320 --> 03:51.412
Same with the roads. The road sections were pretty close. Some of them varied a

03:51.912 --> 03:55.804
little bit, and I don't know how much we'd be supportive of those. Again,

03:55.820 --> 03:58.319
I think, especially when this is a clean slate, I don't— I don't really see

03:58.819 --> 04:01.611
a huge reason not to follow what our standards are for roads.

04:02.595 --> 04:06.225
Yes, you might lose some units, but those

04:06.725 --> 04:07.946
road sections are in our code for a reason.

04:13.805 --> 04:16.868
There's really— I don't think they're planning on building on any of the steep slopes

04:17.368 --> 04:19.316
here on the bench. Given it is a bench, that is a concern, but I

04:19.332 --> 04:22.038
think they've done a good job of avoiding most of those areas.

04:32.212 --> 04:34.936
And I can't speak to a lot of the, the utility concerns that were raised

04:35.436 --> 04:37.764
by our Public Works and Engineering departments, but, um,

04:37.844 --> 04:41.270
of course water is going to be a concern,

04:41.351 --> 04:44.619
right? Can— do we have enough water for this development here?

04:44.846 --> 04:47.111
Do we have enough that we can get there? Yes, they're going to put in

04:47.611 --> 04:50.978
tanks is what they're proposing. But that's definitely a

04:51.478 --> 04:55.774
concern, and I don't know if we have the sewer capacity yet to

04:55.807 --> 04:59.210
handle that. Yes, they'll put new lines

04:59.243 --> 05:02.799
in, but it's— there's still a lot to plan out and to consider as

05:03.299 --> 05:06.685
this proposal goes forward. So that being

05:06.717 --> 05:10.425
said, I'm happy to answer if you have any questions for me. If not,

05:10.748 --> 05:12.360
the applicant's here and ready to present.

05:15.549 --> 05:19.202
Any questions for staff before we talk to the applicant?

05:28.395 --> 05:31.822
Uh, let's go ahead and hear what the applicant has to say.

05:44.040 --> 05:46.766
Thanks so much for having us. My name is Tom Romney.

05:47.510 --> 05:51.170
I'm with Focus Consulting. I'm a planner and civil engineer of

05:51.202 --> 05:53.860
20 years, landscape architect. So,

05:55.470 --> 05:58.660
yeah, so I think we really appreciate getting to this

05:59.160 --> 06:02.612
point, being able to have a work session. So I think for me,

06:02.628 --> 06:06.082
the biggest thing today is we do want to walk through kind of our vision

06:06.582 --> 06:08.768
for this project and how we got to where we got to and why we

06:09.268 --> 06:12.520
got to where we got to. And staff has been great. I really

06:12.552 --> 06:16.502
like working with your staff. A lot of their comments make

06:17.002 --> 06:19.488
sense, and I've actually been working on this for 3 years, so some of it

06:19.617 --> 06:23.246
is just the fact that I think things have tweaked and changed. And so

06:23.551 --> 06:26.023
I don't think any of those are real big-ticket items. So,

06:26.906 --> 06:29.700
and so my goal is to just let you guys kind of know the starting

06:29.716 --> 06:33.554
vision. I think, you know, anytime you're looking at 1,800 acres, we recognize

06:33.650 --> 06:36.955
this is a process with the city. I love work sessions at

06:37.455 --> 06:41.144
this point and the chance to kind of

06:41.419 --> 06:44.460
let you know where we're coming from. And obviously a big thing about today is

06:44.557 --> 06:48.436
hearing kind of your feedback and thoughts and trying to

06:48.936 --> 06:52.107
keep things moving forward because I think our goal is

06:52.204 --> 06:53.894
to have a really successful project.

06:56.357 --> 07:01.609
Working with, you know, kind of I guess as the representative for The

07:02.109 --> 07:06.058
property owner, I know that she has an elevated hope for

07:06.558 --> 07:09.358
this. Like the bench is a really big deal and what it can do for

07:09.858 --> 07:13.552
Eagle Mountain. And so not taking lightly like the size and

07:13.713 --> 07:18.250
what it can be and being very intentional about what

07:18.750 --> 07:22.299
goes there and how we plan for it. And so I

07:22.363 --> 07:26.322
guess in my opinion, we're not in a massive hurry.

07:26.662 --> 07:29.963
Obviously we want to get things moving now. But we want

07:30.463 --> 07:32.668
to work together with the city and try to come up with the best plan

07:33.168 --> 07:37.168
possible. So, I'm just going to kind of walk through some different

07:37.216 --> 07:40.281
things. So, I think everyone's pretty

07:40.346 --> 07:43.911
well aware, but this is the bench. It's 1,800 acres. Most of

07:44.411 --> 07:48.214
it is contiguous, but there's the piece up Wiley Canyon and a piece a little

07:48.714 --> 07:50.996
bit to the north and to the south,

07:52.144 --> 07:55.618
to the southwest. But it's pretty well connected,

07:55.651 --> 07:59.444
1,800 acres. Just to the east of

07:59.944 --> 08:03.555
here. So this is just for context and kind of its relationship

08:03.571 --> 08:07.258
to City Hall and the rest of the city. So this is our bubble

08:07.758 --> 08:11.036
plan. Just some things to note

08:11.536 --> 08:15.292
about this. The really cool thing about this

08:15.792 --> 08:19.080
is you have one property owner that controls 1,800 acres, which is really unique for

08:19.580 --> 08:22.851
Utah. You don't find that very often. Even large

08:23.351 --> 08:26.612
master plans that we generally work on probably top off Not

08:27.112 --> 08:29.706
to say it's not possible, but 900 acres,

08:30.283 --> 08:33.457
you know, maybe sometimes 1,200, 1,000, but even that's pretty rare.

08:33.650 --> 08:36.840
Utah just traditionally is cut up into so many different property owners,

08:37.818 --> 08:40.896
and it gets really challenging to master plan. I've seen it happen.

08:40.944 --> 08:44.487
I've seen master plans happen with multiple property owners, but I just

08:44.987 --> 08:47.581
had one in West Jordan that completely blew up because there were just too many

08:47.677 --> 08:50.979
entities involved, and it didn't work out.

08:51.380 --> 08:54.796
And so That was unfortunate. So I think this is unique.

08:55.294 --> 08:59.358
And so we understand that the city has the 200-acre city

08:59.471 --> 09:03.023
code that master plans aren't supposed to be over 200 acres.

09:03.088 --> 09:05.824
But I just want to talk a little bit through. And I think, you know,

09:05.840 --> 09:10.734
if logistically there's a way to have 9 different master plans and

09:11.234 --> 09:14.810
9 different applications, I think that's fine. But I think I

09:14.826 --> 09:17.324
would hate to give up all the benefits of a large master plan.

09:18.265 --> 09:20.601
just because of city code. And so I kind of want to put that a

09:21.101 --> 09:23.410
little bit on the back burner and try to figure out how to do that

09:23.910 --> 09:27.889
and talk through why 1,800 acres is so valuable as far as master

09:28.389 --> 09:32.127
planning. Whether people like Daybreak or not, something like Daybreak

09:32.223 --> 09:35.305
couldn't happen if you didn't have a large enough landmass

09:35.835 --> 09:38.435
that one entity is planning all at one time.

09:38.997 --> 09:42.079
So I think for us, there's just so many advantages

09:42.176 --> 09:45.049
for the city to master plan something this large with one entity.

09:45.746 --> 09:49.537
Versus cutting it up into multiple small projects

09:50.037 --> 09:53.562
that gets planned over the next 30 years. So,

09:53.724 --> 09:57.225
and then one unique thing, just working with staff, we heard that loud and clear

09:57.338 --> 10:00.743
when they started, that they don't want a master plan that completely

10:01.243 --> 10:06.204
changes the city code and has its own code and that they have to manage

10:06.544 --> 10:10.554
the master plan completely different from the city code. So our intention here

10:10.667 --> 10:13.267
and the reason the bubble plan is what it is was to kind of say,

10:13.284 --> 10:16.796
hey, like, And I wouldn't fixate too much on the

10:17.296 --> 10:21.035
location of anything, 'cause from a planning standpoint, the more flexibility

10:21.067 --> 10:25.065
you have to put a mixture of product together

10:25.081 --> 10:27.971
in different locations and kind of getting away from, hey,

10:28.132 --> 10:31.857
all of our 10,000 square foot lots are here,

10:32.692 --> 10:36.401
all of our acre lots are here, all of our townhomes are

10:36.901 --> 10:40.351
here, the more you can intermix a community, And I'll talk a little bit more

10:40.399 --> 10:43.851
about this and have housing for every stage of life,

10:43.867 --> 10:46.918
but have that community. And you've seen that. I feel like Eagle Mountain has actually

10:47.418 --> 10:50.097
done a really good job of having this, more so than other cities.

10:51.093 --> 10:54.850
I live in Sandy and it's very much, not completely, you'll find pockets,

10:54.866 --> 10:58.190
but it's kind of split up of, hey, if you want this house, you live

10:58.254 --> 11:01.465
in this area. If you want this house, you live in this area. And it's

11:01.965 --> 11:04.500
not awesome. And so just,

11:05.382 --> 11:08.735
We want to provide housing for every stage of

11:09.235 --> 11:13.065
life, but we want to follow the city's current code

11:13.565 --> 11:17.203
of how that happens. And so you'll notice on our bubble plan, we have agricultural,

11:17.652 --> 11:20.556
we have all zones that already exist in the city.

11:21.117 --> 11:24.357
So our plan is to follow those zones, but what we want to do is

11:24.389 --> 11:27.966
intermix those zones. And so that's the goal of

11:28.014 --> 11:31.911
the project. And you'll still have, you know, like housing together,

11:31.927 --> 11:35.800
but not in such large pockets. Thank you. And I'm not going to go

11:36.300 --> 11:39.910
back to it, but even in the in

11:40.250 --> 11:44.025
kind of that table that showed setbacks and lot sizes,

11:44.540 --> 11:48.444
our plan is to keep the majority of those

11:48.556 --> 11:52.171
numbers the same as the city code. I think, and maybe it's changed since

11:52.671 --> 11:55.605
I first put this together since we kind of started a while ago, but the

11:55.621 --> 11:58.926
goal was to have the majority stay the same, and we were asking for a

11:59.426 --> 12:03.555
couple minor changes. So. We did only want to change things in

12:04.055 --> 12:08.203
a few areas and make it easy on staff and have this really follow

12:08.703 --> 12:11.987
the city code, but still be a master plan. So that's kind of the reasoning

12:12.019 --> 12:15.355
why it's named the same things as what's in the city code.

12:17.876 --> 12:21.038
Anything— sorry, I'm going to go back really quick. Anything that's the light green

12:21.538 --> 12:25.474
is like preserved natural open space. And they

12:25.974 --> 12:30.447
showed the slope map. We're staying outside of anything that's 25% Steep

12:30.947 --> 12:34.159
or above. So kind of back to just the

12:34.659 --> 12:37.920
benefits of 1,800 acres, like obviously

12:38.356 --> 12:41.740
we're looking to develop housing. We want to

12:41.805 --> 12:44.608
be very smart and intentional about how we develop housing.

12:46.264 --> 12:50.274
There's a lot of different ways to approach it, but I think as

12:50.371 --> 12:54.413
part of that, we want a community that people want to live in and

12:54.445 --> 12:57.448
that they can live in throughout their life, and not that everyone will do that.

12:58.373 --> 13:02.499
But in order to do that, we, we really started this project considering open space

13:02.692 --> 13:06.721
and Lake Mountain and the natural amenity that already exists,

13:07.026 --> 13:11.425
and the fact that with 1,800 acres you can build regional-type amenity

13:11.457 --> 13:14.588
facilities. And so we'll talk a little bit more about that. But I

13:15.088 --> 13:18.200
think that's the real win for the city, is one, you can provide housing for

13:18.216 --> 13:20.688
not just one group of people, but with that,

13:21.635 --> 13:25.552
by developing 1,800 acres, you're not just developing amenities

13:25.728 --> 13:29.002
for just this neighborhood. You're able to develop amenities

13:29.098 --> 13:32.243
for the larger population of Eagle Mountain and on

13:32.743 --> 13:35.854
top of attracting people to want to live here for that reason of amenities when

13:36.354 --> 13:40.026
you're talking trails and community and regional open space,

13:40.090 --> 13:43.942
not just like pockets to those neighborhoods. And so I

13:44.442 --> 13:47.135
think to me those are— that's really— and then I think the other thing is

13:47.167 --> 13:50.473
just the ability to plan infrastructure. So there's— we're gonna have to work with city

13:50.973 --> 13:54.536
staff on water, sewer, transportation, But when we're looking at 1,800

13:55.036 --> 13:58.537
acres, we've added roads, collector roads that aren't on the city master plan,

13:58.633 --> 14:01.960
just recognizing like, hey, this is a lot of land, this is a lot of

14:02.008 --> 14:05.398
people. How are they going to move through the development beyond just

14:05.898 --> 14:09.689
purely what the current city master plan is asking for?

14:10.299 --> 14:13.513
We've already done a lot of water analysis. So we're going to— again, there's a

14:14.013 --> 14:17.450
lot still to be done, but we recognize 2 additional tanks need to be

14:17.950 --> 14:21.191
built. We've already analyzed this unit count

14:21.224 --> 14:24.338
based on the current city model and

14:24.435 --> 14:27.775
what's happening there. And then, you know, we recognize

14:27.887 --> 14:31.484
that it's a process to work through water shares and

14:31.500 --> 14:35.064
those kind of things. But same with sewer, water, storm drain. By doing

14:35.080 --> 14:39.367
1,800 acres, we can make sure it's planned cohesively instead of kind

14:39.867 --> 14:43.704
of piecemealed every little bit. And I think Everyone gets

14:44.204 --> 14:47.297
a little nervous about the size of something like this, but just recognizing this is

14:47.394 --> 14:51.325
a, you know, 20 to 50 year build out potentially. Like this

14:51.422 --> 14:54.677
is gonna happen over a long period of time.

14:56.927 --> 14:59.338
We did look at Eagle Mountain strategic goals,

15:00.406 --> 15:03.917
and so, you know, some of them are kind of complicated,

15:04.510 --> 15:07.967
but economic growth, I think, you know, obviously this isn't

15:08.467 --> 15:12.056
meant to have. Commercial specifically, not to say that it couldn't, and we've even

15:12.120 --> 15:15.601
considered some ways to implement that into this

15:15.649 --> 15:19.547
area. I think a lot comes down to just density

15:19.659 --> 15:23.749
and houses and, you know, is there potential for additional commercial in this

15:23.814 --> 15:27.744
area? That's not like the focus of the bench. I don't think anyone has seen

15:28.244 --> 15:31.577
it as the commercial zone, but it also, depending on what we do on

15:31.625 --> 15:35.202
the bench, can definitely support what's happening in Eagle Mountain from a commercial

15:35.702 --> 15:39.229
standpoint, from a workforce standpoint. So that's definitely something

15:39.729 --> 15:43.962
we're mindful of. And then more importantly, the other goals that are specifically

15:44.462 --> 15:47.715
related is just the generational planning. I'll talk about that

15:48.215 --> 15:52.737
infrastructure network, service enhancement, unique reputational

15:53.411 --> 15:56.715
evolution, which some of these I had to look up what they meant, but it

15:57.215 --> 16:00.341
was very educational. And then interactive environmental stewardship.

16:01.464 --> 16:04.977
So first, just housing for every stage of life. I think whenever

16:05.477 --> 16:08.907
we look as planners at like the most successful areas

16:09.407 --> 16:13.173
and neighborhoods is the ability for people in every stage of life to live in

16:13.673 --> 16:17.246
a community, the ability to move in, to move up maybe 1 or

16:17.746 --> 16:22.138
2 times, and then to move back down. It's really impactful both

16:22.362 --> 16:25.955
on the neighborhood, partly for demographics. Like, I think we've— everyone's probably

16:25.971 --> 16:28.601
lived in a neighborhood— I'm in it right now— when my kids moved in,

16:29.338 --> 16:32.979
there was lots of little kids. Now there's lots of teenagers. And now

16:33.043 --> 16:36.650
they're all moving out, but there's also new kids moving in. And so

16:37.150 --> 16:39.824
if you have enough housing and the right types of housing,

16:40.401 --> 16:43.623
that wave that some neighborhoods experience on

16:44.123 --> 16:47.984
a small scale isn't as impactful. So just, we do want to be mindful

16:48.032 --> 16:51.735
of the benefits of having housing, all housing types.

16:51.959 --> 16:55.743
And so we want half acres and acre lots

16:55.967 --> 17:00.007
in pieces. We want 12,000 and— 1,000. 15,000-square-foot lots,

17:00.088 --> 17:02.575
but we also see the benefit of having some townhomes,

17:03.157 --> 17:07.372
potentially having apartments, and having things like

17:07.872 --> 17:11.900
6,000 and 8,000-square-foot lots and mixing that

17:16.420 --> 17:19.744
Just infrastructure. I'm not going to go deep into the engineering of it,

17:19.761 --> 17:23.473
but just again, we want— the advantage of the 1,800

17:23.973 --> 17:27.153
acres is that ability to plan it all together. to work with your staff,

17:27.634 --> 17:31.575
make sure that everything from an infrastructure

17:32.075 --> 17:35.645
standpoint is planned for, for the units that we're proposing, and that it's done

17:35.725 --> 17:39.170
early on, that we're not waiting until we get to preliminary to

17:39.670 --> 17:43.224
figure out how is water, sewer, stormwater, and roadways

17:43.272 --> 17:46.252
going to work. And, um,

17:46.765 --> 17:50.338
we've already done a lot of traffic impact study to look at the larger

17:50.930 --> 17:53.318
piece, and I'm not going to go into that, but as we move along,

17:53.350 --> 17:56.782
we're happy to provide quite a bit of information related to that.

17:57.477 --> 18:00.871
To me, the most exciting thing about this project, and I didn't put this slide

18:00.919 --> 18:03.231
first, but really where we started is open space.

18:04.213 --> 18:09.946
I think open space in a master-planned community like this has

18:10.060 --> 18:14.389
the most potential for the city to really elevate beyond

18:14.646 --> 18:18.180
what's currently there. So this doesn't include

18:18.680 --> 18:21.860
any of the small pocket things that would go into development. These are the 5

18:22.588 --> 18:26.134
community or regional open space areas

18:26.166 --> 18:29.858
that are improved open space. So this isn't the natural open space. This is areas

18:29.972 --> 18:33.688
where we're proposing an elevated

18:34.188 --> 18:37.608
amenity for this community, but that would also impact the rest of Eagle Mountain.

18:39.432 --> 18:43.209
So you kind of can see them going left to right. So starting to the

18:43.709 --> 18:46.745
left, there's a non-motorized trailhead, a community park,

18:47.147 --> 18:51.123
an equestrian park, a dog park, and a motorized vehicle trailhead.

18:51.188 --> 18:54.390
We've met and talked to a lot of people, different council

18:54.890 --> 18:58.796
members. When we first met about this project, we considered

18:59.296 --> 19:03.046
like a bunch of different needs from the city, things that people in Eagle Mountain

19:03.078 --> 19:07.157
do. And so not just people who are going to move into this neighborhood,

19:07.189 --> 19:10.699
but then how do you like, how do you provide

19:10.731 --> 19:14.081
regional community amenities that would benefit this community

19:14.104 --> 19:17.835
and Eagle Mountain, but then also opportunities to get up into Lake Mountain.

19:19.327 --> 19:22.808
So starting with— and these are just all conceptual, but like we want

19:22.824 --> 19:26.065
to get the dialogue going. And so even though they're conceptual, we put a lot

19:26.565 --> 19:29.884
of thought into it. So this is— and, and, and the location of

19:30.384 --> 19:34.055
them, like we're not set on anything, but we tried to be intentional

19:34.555 --> 19:37.857
about where they go. For example, our non-motorized trailhead is in a totally different side

19:38.357 --> 19:41.499
of the project as the motorized trailhead because those aren't uses that are super compatible.

19:42.573 --> 19:45.836
So, for example, here a non-motorized trailhead for hiking,

19:45.869 --> 19:50.160
mountain biking, you know, having plenty of parking, having a space

19:50.192 --> 19:55.719
where you can get shade. There's bathrooms, kind of pavilion-type

19:56.219 --> 19:59.650
scenario, and then doing some enhancements to trails and working with

20:00.150 --> 20:03.208
the BLM and creating a hub where there's plenty of parking. You're outside of the

20:03.708 --> 20:07.631
neighborhood, you're on a major collector road, and there's plenty of space

20:08.131 --> 20:11.034
to say, hey, you're going to go here to start on your excursion up into

20:11.534 --> 20:15.370
Lake Mountain. You know, with a community this size, this wouldn't necessarily be

20:15.870 --> 20:19.177
the only park, but creating a park that really has intention,

20:19.771 --> 20:23.368
that has pickleball courts, that has, you know, a really large

20:23.465 --> 20:27.592
regional-type playground. Eagle Mountain already has Quarry Ride, which is an amazing park.

20:27.673 --> 20:31.367
It's honestly one of the best in Utah, you know, but that's not going

20:31.867 --> 20:34.546
to be able to— and I'm not saying this is that size, but it's this

20:35.046 --> 20:38.408
would be substantial. This is something that people beyond this development would

20:38.908 --> 20:41.532
want to go to and be a part of. And we're also trying to be

20:42.032 --> 20:45.554
very mindful of like, yeah, we're going to have like some ball fields

20:46.019 --> 20:49.272
and a playground, but what doesn't need to be sod is not going

20:49.288 --> 20:52.813
to be sod. So we're going to be on the bench. We're going to be

20:53.313 --> 20:56.899
very mindful of how to landscape to preserve water as much as possible.

20:58.197 --> 21:02.141
We know that equestrian is important to Eagle Mountain City. This is

21:02.190 --> 21:05.802
kind of a cool concept that I think is really unique

21:06.300 --> 21:09.319
to have a trailhead for equestrian with plenty of trailer parking.

21:10.699 --> 21:14.168
But then what we're proposing is to actually have like a barn where people

21:14.668 --> 21:17.796
can temporarily house horses. So like whether you're getting ready to go on a trail

21:18.296 --> 21:21.762
ride, if you had people staying overnight in the area, there's ways

21:21.795 --> 21:24.508
to even— I don't know if it would be free or you would rent it

21:25.008 --> 21:28.619
out, but then, you know, building in the water and the facilities to like not

21:29.119 --> 21:31.188
just go to a parking lot and get on your horse and go on a

21:31.688 --> 21:35.525
trail ride, but like— have, similar to

21:36.025 --> 21:38.995
a bike park, an actual equestrian park. So what you see there is like a

21:39.495 --> 21:42.740
rectangular riding pen that would be for the

21:43.240 --> 21:46.518
public, but then also for the neighborhood. We would want to surround this by the

21:47.018 --> 21:50.913
equestrian lots. And so, you know, and to that point,

21:50.977 --> 21:54.081
maybe, you know, there's— there would be details to figure out of how

21:54.194 --> 21:57.460
this functioned. But this was exciting to think of, like, how could you create an

21:57.960 --> 22:01.246
equestrian park even this kind of like zone where, you know,

22:01.311 --> 22:04.332
whether it's new riders or kids or people are able to like,

22:05.040 --> 22:08.590
you know, very similar to a bike park, like on a horse, go through

22:09.090 --> 22:12.527
like an obstacle course, have it there for more than just

22:13.027 --> 22:16.296
the people who have equestrian lots. But then also this would be a trailhead and

22:16.328 --> 22:22.922
this would connect up into Lake Mountain as like your equestrian trailhead

22:23.520 --> 22:27.201
to go on a ride. I was talking

22:27.233 --> 22:30.529
to someone who— I'm not a horse person, so I had to go talk to

22:31.029 --> 22:33.935
a lot of equestrian people. I would love to be a horse person, but allergies

22:33.967 --> 22:37.610
and other things get in the way. But he was like, this would be incredible

22:38.110 --> 22:41.381
because he can go on a ride and his wife can practice barrels and

22:41.413 --> 22:44.938
do the things in the kind of

22:45.035 --> 22:48.833
like outdoor arena. And so I just thought it was a unique

22:48.914 --> 22:51.969
opportunity to like get more creative than just having a trailhead. Thank you.

22:53.795 --> 22:56.569
You know, I think dogs are an important part in a lot of people's lives,

22:56.682 --> 23:00.033
and it's tricky to have a dog park that's big enough. So I think the

23:00.533 --> 23:03.962
unique part about this master plan is we have a lot of land. And so

23:04.394 --> 23:08.371
developing a dog park that's big enough, that is meaningful,

23:08.708 --> 23:12.444
and a place that you'd want to go, and it's large enough to have large

23:12.540 --> 23:16.693
dogs running around and small dogs running around— this one specifically

23:16.886 --> 23:19.291
could go a lot of places and look really different.

23:20.905 --> 23:23.935
But as an example of kind of the size and scale of what we would

23:24.435 --> 23:28.023
want to do for a dog park. And then I think also unique to

23:28.523 --> 23:31.138
Eagle Mountain and Lake Mountain is just motorized vehicles,

23:32.865 --> 23:35.980
giving them a place. I think again, getting them on a road

23:36.480 --> 23:40.735
that people aren't driving through your neighborhood, but giving them a place

23:40.945 --> 23:43.728
and access like Lake Mountain. I'm going to talk about it in a second.

23:43.776 --> 23:47.057
It's a pretty amazing feature. Thank you. that Eagle Mountain has that I think is

23:47.555 --> 23:51.026
slightly an untapped recreational resource for

23:51.526 --> 23:55.027
Eagle Mountain and Saratoga Springs. I feel like the bench is kind

23:55.527 --> 23:58.819
of that opening up Eagle Mountain to the city.

23:59.703 --> 24:03.640
And so a motorized vehicle trailhead that we

24:04.282 --> 24:07.962
can't like see all the detail, but that has plenty of parking. We've even considered

24:08.462 --> 24:11.047
like a little car wash, like a self-service car wash that's part of it.

24:11.624 --> 24:14.927
And just trying to get creative that way, having fire pits and picnic areas.

24:15.521 --> 24:18.952
And so it's not just about a gravel parking lot where you park your trailer

24:19.452 --> 24:22.159
and you take off, but a place where you can— you'd want to be as

24:22.659 --> 24:26.424
you get ready and you come back and making it more meaningful than what I

24:26.924 --> 24:29.454
feel like typically people see in something like a UTV.

24:30.753 --> 24:35.050
These are— this was our kind of amenity plan, but we're open to suggestions

24:35.082 --> 24:38.770
and ideas and, you know, maybe opportunities to elevate it

24:39.270 --> 24:43.729
more. So those are kind of the planned

24:44.229 --> 24:47.451
amenities as of right now. So Lake Mountain vision, I just

24:47.563 --> 24:50.862
always like to remind everybody, I, that like

24:51.362 --> 24:54.789
how awesome it is and just some interesting things.

24:56.100 --> 24:59.877
So, and I've mountain biked a bunch more on

24:59.909 --> 25:03.510
the north side of Lake Mountain, but like I think some cool

25:04.010 --> 25:06.786
things is that Just the amount,

25:07.508 --> 25:11.054
the amount of acres that Lake Mountain is and the amount

25:11.554 --> 25:15.274
of elevation. So Lake Mountain's like 3

25:15.774 --> 25:19.173
to 4 times the size of Corner Canyon as far as like preserved

25:19.285 --> 25:23.168
open space area that's either preserved by the BLM or

25:23.376 --> 25:27.035
the Forest Service. And then I think the most

25:27.484 --> 25:30.853
like kind of spectacular thing to me is the elevation from

25:30.917 --> 25:34.961
the bench. So this project to the peak of Lake Mountain is

25:35.461 --> 25:38.862
about 3,000 feet of elevation. And so Corner Canyon,

25:38.878 --> 25:42.698
for example, is about—

25:43.198 --> 25:46.808
what was that? It's like 1,200 feet of elevation from top

25:47.308 --> 25:50.821
to bottom. And Park City Mountain Resort is about 4,000

25:51.321 --> 25:55.058
feet of elevation from top to bottom. And so just the difference between

25:55.090 --> 25:58.381
Park City Mountain Resort to the top and Lake Mountain is

25:58.397 --> 26:01.479
a pretty minimal difference. So obviously the snow is totally different.

26:02.025 --> 26:06.055
But I've just— I think between Eagle Mountain and Saratoga Springs,

26:06.087 --> 26:09.218
this project's exciting if it's done right and planned right,

26:09.844 --> 26:13.441
because it's an amenity that, you know, we are going

26:13.473 --> 26:17.102
to want to have discussions with the BLM and figure out how to make Lake

26:17.602 --> 26:21.019
Mountain more of an amenity for Eagle Mountain. I think it already is. I think

26:21.519 --> 26:24.889
the challenge is getting to it, having the parking, having the facilities and the trailheads.

26:25.338 --> 26:28.549
But I still think even there's work that could be done to really have the

26:29.049 --> 26:32.372
hiking trails, the biking trails, the UTV trails that feel

26:32.822 --> 26:36.244
purposeful and allow the BLM to preserve what they're trying

26:36.357 --> 26:40.102
to preserve and then also allow people to

26:40.135 --> 26:43.843
use it for recreation. I feel like for Eagle Mountain, you guys

26:43.875 --> 26:46.087
have grown so fast and I know that's a challenge,

26:48.618 --> 26:51.502
but no one should have to go to Corner Canyon from Eagle Mountain.

26:52.101 --> 26:55.893
You have this amazing opportunity right there and so getting it to

26:56.393 --> 26:59.680
where Or even to the Wasatch, right? Like, it would be awesome to be in

26:59.728 --> 27:03.161
Eagle Mountain and feel like for a mountain experience you don't

27:03.194 --> 27:07.326
need to drive outside of your city. So that's the basis

27:07.826 --> 27:11.221
of how we— there's, you know, there's a lot of other details. We've, you know,

27:11.237 --> 27:14.734
we know that there's like the mule deer corridor, and we've

27:15.234 --> 27:19.436
even had discussions and are planning to work with

27:19.936 --> 27:22.472
them to make sure the fencing is correct and that all of those kind of

27:22.504 --> 27:24.992
details have been considered. But I think on a bigger picture,

27:25.089 --> 27:29.537
there's Just things that probably need to get worked through and that we

27:29.553 --> 27:32.755
want to work with the city to get this figured out. So anyways,

27:32.933 --> 27:36.686
that's where we're at today, and we would love as many questions, feedback,

27:37.013 --> 27:38.043
comments that you have.

27:41.602 --> 27:45.225
Any questions? Yeah, I think there's

27:45.725 --> 27:49.525
some quick questions to ask, but one of the things I noticed is the

27:50.025 --> 27:53.176
City Council has recently been having discussions regarding development and

27:53.676 --> 27:56.837
water rights and potentially having those brought forward at the beginning

27:57.255 --> 28:00.305
of the, um, when they have the,

28:00.321 --> 28:04.030
like, the master development plan put in place or at the preliminary plat

28:04.530 --> 28:08.028
stage, straight at the beginning, having those allocated to what's

28:08.528 --> 28:12.058
being approved. It's just a discussion at this point, of course,

28:12.299 --> 28:15.799
not required now, but— For sure. But I mean, if that's something that they want

28:16.299 --> 28:19.160
to do, and I know we're capped at 280 acres kind of per— for developments

28:19.208 --> 28:22.751
and stuff. Would it make sense, and would you— they'd be

28:23.251 --> 28:26.246
able to do it to have that water available to put forward at the beginning

28:26.278 --> 28:30.183
and maybe do it in like 200-acre phases or something

28:30.216 --> 28:34.071
so it's more, so they could actually potentially comply with that and show that they

28:34.571 --> 28:36.906
have what they need so the developer's not just sitting there forever like a lot

28:37.406 --> 28:40.693
of what we've got in our city? Um,

28:40.907 --> 28:43.678
so yeah, so, um, they have water rights.

28:45.007 --> 28:48.659
I don't think anyone has 1,800 acres worth of water rights. And I

28:48.948 --> 28:52.384
guess my opinion of it is it's important to understand

28:52.884 --> 28:56.333
what it is and what needs to happen, because I've seen all developments,

28:56.414 --> 29:00.861
not just in Eagle Mountain. I think that requirement

29:00.909 --> 29:04.811
is going to shut down— like, it's going to financially

29:04.843 --> 29:08.150
burden development to the point where it's going to become really challenging to

29:08.650 --> 29:12.052
develop in Eagle Mountain. Yeah, I'd agree. Yeah. So I think that's like, we're not

29:12.613 --> 29:17.125
against trying to figure it out, but like where I

29:17.625 --> 29:21.509
think that's what's tricky is the advantage of master planning 1,800 acres

29:21.621 --> 29:24.897
is you guys get, it's not just about the developer, the city gets to say,

29:24.913 --> 29:28.317
well, okay, well, if we give you this, we want, if we give you X,

29:28.349 --> 29:31.721
we get Y. And so it allows that chance to come to the table and

29:32.221 --> 29:35.590
say, how do we develop this thoughtfully that benefits both groups? And it's,

29:36.152 --> 29:40.327
It's a successful community, just this development that's

29:40.827 --> 29:44.052
supporting what Eagle Mountain already is. And so my fear

29:44.165 --> 29:47.456
with the water conversation is, well, to actually execute

29:47.956 --> 29:51.438
this development, and we want to do it so that it's continually growing, that it's

29:51.487 --> 29:55.228
never sitting, right? Like there's, there's strategy to having

29:55.661 --> 29:58.792
multiple products in a neighborhood so that the neighborhood can

29:59.292 --> 30:02.934
develop in a timely fashion. But it's something this size

30:03.047 --> 30:06.353
would take at least 30 years to develop. And so to

30:06.853 --> 30:10.206
secure water rights today for 30 years from now, there's just like— Yeah,

30:10.736 --> 30:13.609
it becomes difficult because you got to use them or you lose them kind of

30:13.641 --> 30:17.959
thing. Yeah. And so then it's hard to say,

30:17.975 --> 30:21.474
well, should we not master plan the best long-term

30:21.587 --> 30:24.893
thing for the city because of that? But I do kind of like the phasing

30:24.941 --> 30:28.697
idea of— I mean, it's going to take 30 years anyway, so bringing

30:28.730 --> 30:32.310
them in, piece by piece through the development. So you're not coming

30:32.342 --> 30:35.602
in and trying to approve 1,800 acres all at one time, but doing it in

30:36.340 --> 30:39.471
kind of phasing it makes a lot of sense too, I think. Yeah,

30:39.953 --> 30:43.485
which our goal would be to phase it from a preliminary standpoint.

30:43.903 --> 30:47.708
The tricky part is if we don't get a master plan approved, we can't commit

30:47.837 --> 30:51.433
to the same level of amenity packages. I think that's what,

30:51.915 --> 30:55.191
you know, you could just follow the city code and say, okay, well, We're going

30:55.255 --> 30:58.884
to rezone each 200 acres. The reason that's a little tricky

30:58.900 --> 31:01.453
is the zone is set up like typical zoning,

31:02.047 --> 31:05.322
and I'm not saying you couldn't, but you'd have to— it's hard to cut it

31:05.822 --> 31:08.213
into pieces and then you have to say, okay, well, these homes are going exactly

31:08.713 --> 31:12.146
here and these there. And so that's where it gets challenging, is if we spread

31:12.646 --> 31:16.128
it out over 30 years coming at 200-acre pieces with the water at that

31:17.140 --> 31:20.143
point, which is probably more financially feasible.

31:20.933 --> 31:24.538
it gets pretty much impossible to say, okay, well,

31:24.586 --> 31:28.852
we're committing to all these same amenities and this same overall infrastructure

31:29.352 --> 31:33.151
plan. Yeah, I do know there's been some conversation,

31:33.247 --> 31:35.653
I guess, about having different, like, kind of like what you guys are doing a

31:36.153 --> 31:39.727
little bit where you're clustering different property types together so it's not like all townhomes.

31:39.824 --> 31:43.978
Yeah. And then trying to mix in some of this affordable housing

31:44.074 --> 31:47.539
with these large estate lots or whatever it is they wanna

31:48.039 --> 31:51.522
do. And so I know they're, City Council is favorable to some of that

31:51.570 --> 31:55.346
design a little bit? I think definitely. Usually with stuff like this goes development

31:55.846 --> 31:59.234
agreements. And what I've typically seen in Utah lately is in the development agreement,

31:59.813 --> 32:03.219
there's a commitment of like, yeah, we either need to provide the water or we

32:03.719 --> 32:07.204
need to purchase the water. And it's way easier to do that at the time

32:07.704 --> 32:11.318
that you're going— like, as soon as you're going to preliminary

32:11.818 --> 32:15.622
platting or final platting on a project, it's— that's when It's really

32:15.879 --> 32:19.376
easy at that point to say, okay, like, we're going, we're going to start building

32:19.424 --> 32:22.553
this year, we're going to secure the water, dedicate it

32:23.053 --> 32:25.745
to the city, purchase it at the same time. So would you be looking at,

32:25.825 --> 32:29.098
um, putting like all the infrastructure in

32:29.194 --> 32:31.665
just as, as you go along, or trying to just get the whole thing,

32:31.857 --> 32:35.162
like lay everything down and then just kind of build the lots in? Something this

32:35.662 --> 32:39.429
size, you, you want to be purposeful, so you wouldn't put everything

32:39.477 --> 32:42.880
in at once because you don't want roads that that are sitting, that get turned

32:42.928 --> 32:45.513
over to the city that you have to maintain that aren't being used.

32:46.043 --> 32:49.672
But like, we would have a plan of all of, it would be very

32:50.172 --> 32:54.344
similar to the city master plans, or like,

32:54.392 --> 32:57.764
yeah, so like your sewer master plan and your water master plan. Right.

32:57.828 --> 33:01.457
We would have miniature ones just for this development. So we knew like,

33:01.618 --> 33:05.584
okay, we're extending or we're improving Eagle Mountain Boulevard

33:05.632 --> 33:08.921
further east. You know, we're not going to put in any 8-inch water line.

33:08.985 --> 33:12.240
We're going to put in the size of water line that supports the entire build-out.

33:12.545 --> 33:15.239
So that's the difference is like, yeah, build it for the entire— we build in

33:15.271 --> 33:18.927
phases, but we know every master-planned line so that

33:18.959 --> 33:22.567
we're never having to come back and upsize later. Yeah, you're probably familiar

33:23.067 --> 33:25.855
with The Point. It's kind of interesting what the state's required of them. They have

33:26.355 --> 33:29.062
to bring all the utilities into the middle before they build out anything and then

33:29.078 --> 33:32.814
build outward. Interesting. But they've been able to find a way to pencil it

33:33.314 --> 33:36.360
out with some of the Other things and the ways they're developing. But it's a

33:36.860 --> 33:39.410
challenge. And that doesn't— to me, that didn't make a whole lot of sense.

33:39.440 --> 33:41.980
But they didn't want to have a little donut hole in the middle when all

33:42.480 --> 33:46.200
this is built out over decades of time, which would look very weird

33:46.700 --> 33:51.100
and odd. And so that's kind of why they did it that way. But yeah,

33:51.200 --> 33:55.290
I definitely don't think that kind of makes sense. Usually we would build like— yeah,

33:55.540 --> 33:58.720
we would look at what's coming over the next couple of years.

33:59.576 --> 34:03.562
not try to get significant infrastructure in that opens

34:04.062 --> 34:08.738
up neighborhoods. Did you guys have a— I

34:08.771 --> 34:11.697
guess so much state requires so much of it,

34:12.166 --> 34:16.030
what we developed to be affordable housing. Is that tied into this as well

34:16.530 --> 34:19.613
within this development at all? Do you know? That's not something that has been a

34:19.773 --> 34:23.630
major discussion as of now. Nothing yet. Okay. Yeah. I think

34:23.838 --> 34:27.472
obviously the goal by Kind of having pretty

34:27.972 --> 34:32.064
much every housing type is definitely to meet that at some level. But I

34:32.369 --> 34:35.982
think that's definitely something that we're open, would wanna

34:36.303 --> 34:39.899
talk about and see how that fits in. Yeah, and that would

34:39.931 --> 34:43.079
run as a, I guess that would be an HOA, correct? Yeah. For the

34:43.579 --> 34:45.471
development, and then you'd have some of those— I don't know that it has to

34:45.971 --> 34:48.698
be an HOA. You just have to have deed restricted. In order to meet affordable

34:49.198 --> 34:52.745
housing, you just have to have deed restrictions. Sure. And so, and then, and then

34:52.937 --> 34:55.811
a lot of those amenities would still be open to the, the general public,

34:55.827 --> 34:58.926
I guess. Oh yeah. Based on what you were saying before. So yeah, the intention

34:58.942 --> 35:02.539
of these amenities is that they would, um, I think as

35:02.587 --> 35:06.184
of today in that, you know, but is that they would be public

35:06.489 --> 35:11.017
amenities. It's kind of the same way Firefly's doing it too. So yeah.

35:12.269 --> 35:15.176
Any other questions? I just have a comment.

35:15.738 --> 35:19.558
Okay. I really like the overall plan, and I really

35:19.654 --> 35:23.698
like how thoughtful that you guys are being, trying to appeal to

35:23.987 --> 35:27.774
different types of people, whether they're motorized people or equestrian

35:28.274 --> 35:31.867
people or sports people with open lands. I really like

35:31.931 --> 35:35.911
that, and I like the thought of continuity for

35:36.103 --> 35:39.698
all of the areas as you're building this out over the next 30 to 40

35:39.730 --> 35:43.325
years. Um, figuring out how to do that is going to be a

35:43.680 --> 35:46.431
challenge, but I do, I do respect it and I like it.

35:47.790 --> 35:51.455
Thanks so much. Yeah, Commissioner Allen.

35:51.792 --> 35:54.732
Yeah, I can tell it's been very thought out and you know our city well

35:55.342 --> 35:59.053
based on its design. I think it's great for this part

35:59.553 --> 36:02.782
of the city. I think it'll mirror very well Firefly on the southwest corner with

36:02.798 --> 36:06.414
the bike trails, and I think it does add to our city. I can tell,

36:06.655 --> 36:10.766
like I said, you know our city with the equestrian stuff, the bikes, the off-road

36:10.798 --> 36:14.330
vehicles. Like, that's really cool. So I appreciate all that work. I think this fits

36:14.830 --> 36:18.168
Eagle Mountain very, very well. Um, as far as some things

36:18.668 --> 36:20.897
on here, like the roads, I think, yeah, if we can stick to our code,

36:20.914 --> 36:24.382
like you said, it's probably changed. Yeah, that's not a problem. Yeah, go to that.

36:24.590 --> 36:28.621
Um, one thought I had, you'd mentioned with the equestrian park possibly

36:29.121 --> 36:32.249
