37:15.872 --> 37:18.634
and you can kind of see like a dashed orange line or a dashed blue

37:18.746 --> 37:22.536
line. So those will all be preserved open

37:23.036 --> 37:27.000
space corridors that go west to east through the project. So from that equestrian

37:27.032 --> 37:29.617
park, and I'm not saying we wouldn't move it, we could definitely move it.

37:30.262 --> 37:33.881
But like each trailhead that's not on that east boundary will

37:33.929 --> 37:37.327
have that corridor that gets you through the project. So you're not going

37:37.375 --> 37:40.709
through a neighborhood. Perfect. Okay. And it'll border a drainage

37:40.741 --> 37:44.652
we're planning on maintaining anyways. And so you'll have like kind of this natural drainage

37:44.908 --> 37:48.563
and then a trail on one side. Oh, perfect. Okay, cool. And we're even doing

37:48.659 --> 37:51.704
that. So those orange— I didn't really talk a lot about trails, but every collector

37:52.204 --> 37:55.631
road would have a trail. So those kind of red dashed

37:56.131 --> 37:59.510
lines are collector roads, those that have trails on them. That connect you kind

38:00.010 --> 38:04.113
of back into Eagle Mountain, and then the orange is

38:04.947 --> 38:08.747
those orange dotted lines or the blue dotted lines. The blue is for equestrian;

38:08.796 --> 38:11.810
the orange is for hiking or biking.

38:11.923 --> 38:15.146
But those would be like dirt, or yeah, those would be like kind of like

38:15.646 --> 38:19.364
a single track trail that follow those drainage ditches so that people

38:19.444 --> 38:23.325
can get through our project to Lake Mountain. If you're

38:23.825 --> 38:27.206
starting kind of in the utility corridor somewhere, or or just starting

38:27.254 --> 38:30.590
from the city center, you don't have to. take streets if you don't

38:31.090 --> 38:34.490
want to. Nice. Thank you for clarifying that. But I'll still, like,

38:34.990 --> 38:37.890
we can still talk about it. Okay. Our only thought was like trying to keep

38:38.390 --> 38:42.170
as much of the larger amenities on major roadways versus tucking them up into

38:42.670 --> 38:45.790
neighborhoods. Yeah, I like that. Making them easy to access. Yeah, for sure.

38:46.110 --> 38:49.490
And then, uh, one other thought, sprinkling, uh, that's kind of what we're calling it

38:49.990 --> 38:53.030
now in the city, sprinkling the densities and the different housing types. Honestly, I'm all

38:53.530 --> 38:55.290
for it. We've been talking about it more and more because like you said,

38:55.790 --> 38:59.732
if you can Move up within the same neighborhood, the same area. I think

39:00.232 --> 39:02.496
that's awesome because I think it's good instead of having to bounce around the city.

39:02.576 --> 39:06.176
It's good to stay there with your friends, your family. So I'm all for the

39:06.676 --> 39:08.988
sprinkling and however you can do that. And then, Chair, if we have time at

39:09.488 --> 39:11.494
the end of the work session, I'd like to maybe ask staff a few questions

39:11.994 --> 39:15.496
about the 200-acre split thing, have a discussion on that. And I guess just

39:15.996 --> 39:18.308
one other thing I think that will come up is the density. It sounds like

39:18.808 --> 39:21.328
a lot of numbers. It sounds like a lot of units, which it is.

39:21.842 --> 39:25.262
But it is also over 1,800 acres. So just where we got to

39:25.278 --> 39:28.987
that number is 5 units to the acre, which is like

39:29.487 --> 39:32.840
kind of a middle ground of all of— 5 units to

39:33.340 --> 39:36.083
the acre is like R-3 in your residential neighborhood number 1.

39:36.532 --> 39:39.422
So again, we're not going to have that across the whole thing, but it just

39:39.922 --> 39:43.644
provides that ability to have lower density in areas, higher density in areas.

39:44.238 --> 39:48.397
And so that's how the number got devised. I've just found it successful

39:48.897 --> 39:52.504
in master plans that that 5 units to the acre allows you to provide the

39:53.004 --> 39:57.327
different housing types, whether it's really low density or

39:57.455 --> 40:01.525
not. At this point in Eagle Mountain, there's nothing that's that high, but higher density.

40:02.199 --> 40:05.500
And so that's how we arrived at that number. I know,

40:05.644 --> 40:08.801
I noticed on your concept plan you got that, I guess,

40:09.233 --> 40:12.791
southeast area that's got some foothill residential— no,

40:12.855 --> 40:15.867
is it neighborhood? I don't know what it is, what the color is, but it's

40:15.963 --> 40:19.362
got some development in there up on How would that be accessed?

40:19.827 --> 40:23.355
Through an easement through the BLM. Oh, okay. So it's not

40:23.855 --> 40:27.749
ideal, but it could be really cool. Just connecting— where

40:28.249 --> 40:30.764
would that— where would you kind of be looking just as a general idea?

40:31.357 --> 40:34.965
Yeah, so yeah, we would have to connect a roadway through the BLM. Just come

40:35.465 --> 40:39.600
down and— As part of our master plan, we're also contemplating

40:39.616 --> 40:43.047
a lot of things for that piece, but like making it— Wiley Canyon

40:43.144 --> 40:45.575
is really cool. So whatever we do, we want it to,

40:47.511 --> 40:51.867
like, be impactful for the city. And so we'll

40:52.367 --> 40:53.448
have to work with the BLM on a few things.

40:56.523 --> 40:59.790
Did you have some questions? I don't think so. We good?

40:59.967 --> 41:03.169
Anybody else? I got just a couple of questions.

41:05.250 --> 41:08.546
Number of houses, what is your projection for the

41:09.046 --> 41:13.200
total project? Do you have any idea? Yeah, they had it on. It's about 9,000

41:13.700 --> 41:16.370
units. 9,000? Yeah. Okay. I think it was 92-something.

41:18.760 --> 41:21.160
I don't have it on my slides, I don't think, but I have it.

41:21.210 --> 41:24.000
I think you had it on one of those, like 9,120. Okay, yeah. But there

41:24.500 --> 41:26.380
was an extra zero on there, it looked like, on one of those slides.

41:26.960 --> 41:29.980
Yeah, that's not— is that 91,000 or 9,000? I assumed it was 91,000. You missed

41:30.480 --> 41:33.840
a decimal point there. 9,000 is way less than

41:34.340 --> 41:39.115
91,000. So, and then you

41:39.147 --> 41:42.239
mentioned your timings, your projection is out 30 years,

41:42.287 --> 41:45.364
is that correct? Just for like, so yeah, we would like to get started as

41:45.412 --> 41:48.808
soon as we can, but I would say to build the whole project, it's at

41:48.841 --> 41:52.494
least a 30-year project. Okay. And then I guess the

41:52.526 --> 41:55.250
other one I've got is just more of a comment.

41:56.596 --> 42:00.585
I assume you've been working with the wildlife for

42:00.617 --> 42:04.207
wildlife corridors and ways through there. And just

42:04.707 --> 42:08.140
want to make sure we're not forgetting that because that is a critical part

42:08.640 --> 42:12.698
of the wildlife management area. For sure. So what was submitted for a concept plan

42:12.795 --> 42:15.989
is pretty basic just to get this conversation going.

42:16.502 --> 42:20.050
The actual master plan is what is a large document that

42:20.550 --> 42:22.602
shows all of the details related to like, yeah,

42:23.324 --> 42:26.903
we're— we've met with them and are committing

42:26.919 --> 42:29.985
to do the fencing along that perimeter that they want. And we've

42:30.485 --> 42:34.080
even talked about like at a trailhead you have to do some unique things to

42:34.884 --> 42:38.210
manage kind of that corridor versus people getting in

42:38.242 --> 42:42.198
and out. And so we're very aware of their needs and working

42:42.231 --> 42:42.682
with them.

42:45.380 --> 42:48.657
Okay. Any other questions

42:50.039 --> 42:53.847
from— okay. Appreciate it.

42:54.040 --> 42:57.669
Thank you so much. And then we wanted to come back around to

42:58.169 --> 43:00.575
some questions for staff. Yeah, I just wanted to talk a little bit about this

43:00.687 --> 43:04.380
200-acre master plan. I get the reason for it, but I

43:04.880 --> 43:08.875
also love the idea of a master-planned community like

43:09.375 --> 43:13.338
of this size. I think it's great to see the overall idea and structure and

43:13.755 --> 43:16.420
how it's all going to come together like we've done with Firefly. So I was

43:16.920 --> 43:20.241
just curious on, is there a way to do like a hybrid approach,

43:20.273 --> 43:23.146
or what does staff— what are your thoughts on that of like an overall master

43:23.646 --> 43:25.795
plan that maybe we approve in 200-acre chunks at a time, or I don't know?

43:25.960 --> 43:29.760
Just kind of spitballing here. Honestly, I don't

43:30.260 --> 43:32.520
know. I don't have an answer for you. This would be the first one that

43:33.020 --> 43:36.860
this requirement's actually in effect. It's a—

43:37.360 --> 43:38.740
I mean, I don't even know how new the requirement is, probably before I started

43:39.240 --> 43:41.670
here, but we haven't done one of these since then.

43:42.540 --> 43:45.750
So, I mean, ideally, yeah, you follow the requirement,

43:45.800 --> 43:48.620
but the whole purpose usually of a master development plan is sometimes with that agreement

43:49.120 --> 43:52.240
is you can move things around and make it work and

43:52.300 --> 43:56.775
And maybe that's one. And I don't know, I don't know if we want

43:57.275 --> 44:00.470
to do that. That's— So I think the, the reason behind this is it doesn't

44:00.486 --> 44:04.380
mean you can't bring in a project with, in this case, 10 different master

44:04.444 --> 44:08.180
development plan, uh, applications simultaneously,

44:08.679 --> 44:12.472
but we're charging them one fee. But the, the amount of time it

44:12.972 --> 44:16.905
takes staff to review an 1,800-acre-plus master development

44:16.937 --> 44:20.008
plan is not the same as the amount of time it takes to review a

44:20.508 --> 44:23.825
200-acre. So they— this is a way to, I think, assess the

44:24.325 --> 44:27.386
appropriate fee based on the amount of work it requires staff. It's not saying you

44:27.886 --> 44:31.285
can't bring in an 1,800-acre project and file, you know,

44:31.349 --> 44:34.389
10 different— you know, because this is slightly in excess of 1,800 acres,

44:34.971 --> 44:38.689
bring in 10 separate master development

44:39.189 --> 44:43.294
plan applications and have them heard simultaneously as, you know, one cohesive

44:43.794 --> 44:47.289
project. What it is is a way to understand that,

44:47.789 --> 44:51.407
you know, there are added costs associated with

44:51.907 --> 44:55.703
reviewing a long application to make sure the city is actually adequately compensated

44:56.203 --> 44:59.204
for the time being spent reviewing it. Honestly, I think that's the purpose.

45:01.218 --> 45:04.553
There are benefits as we have

45:05.053 --> 45:10.013
it set up. If there is a desire to submit simultaneous

45:10.143 --> 45:12.791
or a group of applications,

45:13.067 --> 45:18.103
staff does see a great benefit to

45:18.603 --> 45:21.693
reviewing infrastructure and other things at a more regional

45:21.806 --> 45:25.143
level. That's one of the things we've

45:25.643 --> 45:29.047
really been trying to tackle and focus even

45:29.547 --> 45:33.048
with some of our code changes is to try to get out

45:33.064 --> 45:38.969
in front of development and promote connectivity

45:38.985 --> 45:41.078
with streets and infrastructure.

45:44.828 --> 45:48.513
Yeah, just, I, I like the idea of looking at the project

45:49.013 --> 45:52.223
as a whole, in my opinion. I know it's a lot of work,

45:52.432 --> 45:56.029
but, uh, at least you don't miss something by

45:56.767 --> 46:00.123
piecing it together 200 acres at

46:00.623 --> 46:03.897
a time, and then all of a sudden, 30, 25 years later, you figured out

46:04.397 --> 46:07.526
you overlooked something. So it— I like

46:08.026 --> 46:12.207
the idea of looking at this big of a project, from infrastructure,

46:12.659 --> 46:15.114
utilities, power,

46:15.744 --> 46:20.488
everything. It, you know, it gives us a chance to plan 30

46:20.504 --> 46:23.674
years ahead. So again, there's nothing in the code that says you can't,

46:24.420 --> 46:27.598
um, that, that you can't bring projects that are more than 200 acres.

46:27.647 --> 46:29.146
It just says you need to file separate applications.

46:31.117 --> 46:33.669
Yeah, so I guess we could think of it as like phasing it, right,

46:33.718 --> 46:36.498
Mark? Is what you're saying we got 1801, we're doing in kind of phases that

46:36.998 --> 46:40.627
are 200 acres or Yeah, you could say this is a master development plan

46:41.127 --> 46:44.298
for these 200 acres. Here's the overall project plan

46:44.491 --> 46:47.550
that we intend to bring before, uh, the council. And if

46:48.050 --> 46:51.186
it makes sense, you could bring them simultaneously and have it reviewed as one cohesive

46:51.686 --> 46:55.228
whole. But I mean, the code requires what it requires, and that's what we need

46:55.728 --> 46:56.085
to follow— our code.

47:00.911 --> 47:02.998
Uh, any other questions? Go ahead.

47:04.588 --> 47:08.405
Thank you. Um, I guess I, I'm less

47:08.533 --> 47:12.446
concerned about sort of the specific mechanics. I get it's probably cleaner for—

47:12.478 --> 47:16.070
in some respects if it's one whole, but I do, just from like the

47:16.570 --> 47:20.304
simple, like we have a resource unlike

47:20.804 --> 47:23.752
maybe any other city that I can think of in the area in terms of

47:24.473 --> 47:27.536
wide open spaces. It's such a shame when they

47:27.616 --> 47:31.339
get like piecemealed together in a, haphazard way. So I, I,

47:31.547 --> 47:35.594
I, kudos to the development in terms of like trying to bring a whole

47:36.043 --> 47:39.720
plan that, that brings in these types of regional amenities that when someone

47:40.220 --> 47:43.285
looks at a valley like the Cedar Valley that we live in, like that's the

47:43.349 --> 47:47.106
one thing we should get right is getting planning to

47:47.606 --> 47:51.457
use this land in appropriate ways. So, um, I really like pieces

47:51.957 --> 47:55.973
of it, whether it has to be 10 applications for our code You

47:56.473 --> 48:00.380
know, I think we can still consider the regional impact

48:00.880 --> 48:04.900
and the benefit that it can be to the city all as one cohesive

48:09.827 --> 48:12.302
Any other questions? That's good.

48:13.224 --> 48:16.787
Well, thank you. And with that,

48:17.599 --> 48:20.635
I guess the next— oh, yes,

48:20.927 --> 48:24.359
please. I didn't mean to skip by you. That's okay.

48:24.728 --> 48:27.470
My name is Amanda Mendenhall. I'm with the landowner.

48:28.176 --> 48:31.046
And so I just want to have some clarifying questions for Marcus so that I

48:31.546 --> 48:34.108
make sure that I understand exactly what we're looking at here.

48:34.685 --> 48:38.373
So, um, my understanding is that even though

48:38.918 --> 48:42.366
planning it on the large level, which makes

48:42.866 --> 48:46.134
more sense because the Phase A, if you're going to require what, you know,

48:47.096 --> 48:50.210
the infrastructure that's going to accommodate you know,

48:50.226 --> 48:53.630
Phase Z. The only way that you can do that

48:54.130 --> 48:56.614
is if we're planning the entire project at the same time.

48:57.388 --> 49:00.067
And so when I'm reading the code, because I did print it out because I

49:00.131 --> 49:03.343
want to make sure that I'm clear as I go back to the landowner,

49:04.489 --> 49:08.346
it talks about how in order to do that phased approach,

49:08.459 --> 49:12.255
even if the applications all come in at the same time, that there

49:12.755 --> 49:16.196
has to be benchmarks before the next one can be approved. Am I reading the

49:16.696 --> 49:17.116
code correctly there?

49:20.401 --> 49:24.006
There are benchmarks that were required in the master development agreement when

49:24.038 --> 49:27.715
you get to that step in the phase. And again,

49:27.908 --> 49:31.794
you can do that based on this

49:32.548 --> 49:36.707
phased approach with the understanding that there is a one whole cohesive project.

49:37.398 --> 49:41.444
So it's 200 with phases? So it's a separate application for

49:41.944 --> 49:45.820
master development plan. But again, the language is any proposed development of more than 200

49:46.320 --> 49:49.057
acres. So it presupposes that developments will exceed 200 acres.

49:49.121 --> 49:52.746
Mm-hmm. In size must divide the project into separate master

49:53.246 --> 49:56.619
development applications of 200 acres or less. Again, those— there's nothing— we

49:57.119 --> 50:00.896
hear applications simultaneously all the time. There's nothing that says we're

50:01.041 --> 50:04.570
ready to file a master development plan application on the full

50:04.603 --> 50:07.989
1,800+ acres. There's nothing saying you can't file that simultaneously.

50:08.696 --> 50:10.806
review together, if that's what you want to do.

50:12.081 --> 50:15.615
Would those time out, though? So if we file them all

50:16.115 --> 50:18.858
at the same time, wouldn't they time out and we'd have to reapply as we

50:19.358 --> 50:23.441
go along? Are you talking about timing out on— If

50:23.941 --> 50:27.168
we go online application— At what stage, exactly? Is one of the applications going to

50:27.668 --> 50:30.780
be good for 20 years? Well, I would hope—

50:31.280 --> 50:34.015
you're talking about— so you're talking about is the approval going to be good for

50:34.515 --> 50:37.921
20 years? Okay, so as far as expiration goes,

50:38.980 --> 50:42.609
you know, and again, you can take this for what it's worth, and I've heard

50:43.109 --> 50:46.768
comments on this before, but it says essentially that you have

50:47.268 --> 50:50.718
6 years to complete the project, which is not realistic for an 1,800,

50:50.734 --> 50:53.961
I'll grant you that, 800-acre project.

50:54.234 --> 50:57.975
And then you can have 2 extensions of 2 years each, so basically 10 years.

50:58.023 --> 51:01.556
Now, if you want to pitch the City Council on why it

51:02.056 --> 51:04.653
should deviate from its code, That is your prerogative,

51:05.326 --> 51:07.427
but that's what the code says. Okay.

51:08.518 --> 51:12.062
And then I do actually want to engage in a water discussion

51:12.562 --> 51:15.494
with you just for a moment because I watched the City Council last week,

51:15.590 --> 51:18.798
and so I do have some opinions on water because obviously

51:19.298 --> 51:22.535
for an 1,800-acre project, there's a lot of water. And when

51:22.567 --> 51:26.127
you're talking about 30 years in time, who knows, right?

51:26.800 --> 51:30.553
And so our proposal had been we We did submit a letter saying

51:30.665 --> 51:34.754
that, you know, we do have adequate capital also to purchase

51:35.254 --> 51:38.475
water as time goes on, because we understand as we start to put money into

51:38.843 --> 51:42.660
the project that we don't know what that's going to look like. And so taking

51:42.692 --> 51:47.053
the water from the landowner is going to only encourage piecemeal

51:47.679 --> 51:51.207
development in your city, especially because we don't know as water

51:51.707 --> 51:54.991
becomes available, as we're searching for water, if the city's selling water again.

51:55.103 --> 51:58.714
You just never know. And so I would just think that

51:59.214 --> 52:02.937
the best policy would be to wait until you're platting, because at that point

52:02.985 --> 52:06.099
it's go. It's not like we're gonna have infrastructure that's

52:06.599 --> 52:10.498
halfway built, because at that point it's been platted. And if we're dedicating a plat,

52:10.514 --> 52:14.046
we're not gonna start buildings and then abandon the project. So anyway,

52:14.384 --> 52:18.285
I think it's an interesting discussion that really the city should consider more carefully.

52:18.477 --> 52:22.844
Yeah, yeah, I think it's a good discussion too. And I can see what they're—

52:23.344 --> 52:27.060
thought and, and kind of intention is behind it. But then you bring

52:27.560 --> 52:29.620
up the idea of if you don't use it, you lose it. And so if

52:30.120 --> 52:32.360
you're bringing all this water at the beginning and you don't use it, then,

52:32.860 --> 52:35.360
you know, you're dealing with a 30-year plan. Like you said, it, it really encourages

52:35.860 --> 52:39.180
those small little chunks of development here and there, which in some ways is

52:39.680 --> 52:41.440
good for our city because we want to slow growth. But at the same time,

52:41.940 --> 52:45.120
if you've already got it, you know, phased like you have it—

52:45.620 --> 52:48.400
it's a 30-year project, we're not just going to build this overnight— and each Each

52:48.432 --> 52:51.973
piece is going to have to be approved as it's platted anyway, right? So I

52:52.021 --> 52:54.249
think, you know, and bringing it on at that time, for me, to me,

52:54.281 --> 52:57.486
makes sense. But, and, and sometimes, um, in, in our

52:57.986 --> 53:01.508
situation, we've, we've actually sold the lots to builders that already have

53:01.556 --> 53:05.226
their own water. And so for us to hypothecate it at the beginning

53:05.258 --> 53:08.367
doesn't make any sense because, you know, there's plenty of builders

53:08.867 --> 53:12.389
out there that actually already have their own water. And so they're purchasing over here

53:12.889 --> 53:15.058
and they end up with extra water that comes with it. And so it doesn't

53:15.558 --> 53:18.738
make any sense, and it kind of— it would slow growth for sure,

53:18.818 --> 53:21.336
and I know that that is partially the goal, but at the same time,

53:21.352 --> 53:24.245
it doesn't encourage thoughtful growth either. So.

53:30.153 --> 53:34.026
Thank you. Agenda review.

53:34.107 --> 53:38.189
Is there anything we need to talk about before— I

53:38.689 --> 53:42.541
think— It's pretty straightforward. So with that,

53:42.573 --> 53:46.475
I will end our workshop, and we will reconvene

53:46.571 --> 53:51.773
in about 5 minutes for our Planning

